# Conventional commits

Commit messages are validated by the [`conventional-msg`](https://github.com/jessedhillon/conventional-msg) pre-commit hook at the `commit-msg` stage. The hook enforces a modified conventional commit format with types, areas, tags, and minimum message length.

## Format

{% raw %}
```
type(area[,...])[!]: [{tag}] message
```
{% endraw %}

| Component      | Required | Description                                                   |
| -------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `type`         | yes      | Commit category (see below)                                   |
| `(area[,...])` | usually  | Affected code area(s), comma-separated, alphabetically sorted |
| `!`            | no       | Breaking change indicator (only with `{tag}`)                 |
| `{tag}`        | no       | Modifier in curly braces (e.g. `{tests-failing}`)             |
| `message`      | yes      | Description, minimum 8 characters                             |

## Types

| Type     | Purpose                   | Area required |
| -------- | ------------------------- | ------------- |
| `feat`   | new feature               | yes           |
| `fix`    | bug fix                   | yes           |
| `chore`  | maintenance, dependencies | yes           |
| `docs`   | documentation changes     | no            |
| `revise` | revise earlier commits    | special       |
| `wip`    | work in progress          | no            |

`test` and `refactor` are NOT allowed types. Use `revise` for refactoring work.

## Areas

Areas are unrestricted by default — any `[\w-]+` value is accepted. Multiple areas are comma-separated and must be **alphabetically sorted**:

```bash
# Good
feat(api,frontend): add user dashboard

# Bad — not sorted
feat(frontend,api): add user dashboard
```

Types listed as "area required: no" (`docs`, `wip`) can omit the area entirely:

```bash
docs: update vault index
wip: checkpoint exploratory spike
```

## The `revise` type

`revise` has special semantics for referencing earlier work.

### With ancestor commit hash

```bash
revise(a1b2c3d): forgot to add config.yaml
```

The area is a git commit hash (6+ hex characters). The hook verifies:

1. The hash refers to an existing commit
2. The commit is an ancestor of HEAD

Use this when revising a specific prior commit that has already been pushed or is on another branch.

### Without scope

```bash
revise: clean up imports after rebase
```

Allowed only on feature branches with 2+ commits from `master`. Not allowed on `master` itself or the first (basal) commit of a branch. Use this for general cleanup on a feature branch that will be squashed.

### With a non-hash area

```bash
revise(storage): rewrite query to avoid N+1
```

Regular area scope — no git validation, just indicates the area being revised.

## Tags

Tags are optional modifiers in curly braces after a breaking change indicator (`!`):

```bash
chore(lib)!: {tests-failing} redefined pi constant
```

Default allowed tags: `tests-failing`.

## Examples

```bash
# Feature with area
feat(api): add user registration endpoint

# Fix with area
fix(storage): handle null timestamps in migration

# Chore with area
chore(deps): update fastapi to 0.115

# Docs without area
docs: update deployment runbook

# Revision referencing ancestor commit
revise(a1b2c3d): add missing test fixture

# Revision on feature branch (no scope)
revise: reorder imports

# WIP without area
wip: experimenting with new pipeline

# Multiple sorted areas
feat(api,frontend): add user profile page
```

## Configuration

The hook reads `[tool.conventional-msg]` from `pyproject.toml` in the repo root. Available options:

| Key               | Default                                         | Description                          |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `types`           | `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `revise`, `wip` | allowed type values                  |
| `areas`           | `None` (unrestricted)                           | allowed area values, `None` = any    |
| `tags`            | `tests-failing`                                 | allowed tag values                   |
| `min_len`         | `8`                                             | minimum message character count      |
| `branch`          | `master`                                        | default branch name for revise logic |
| `allow_omit_area` | `docs`, `wip`                                   | types that don't require an area     |

## Installation

The hook is installed via `install-hooks` (or `pre-commit install --install-hooks`). It runs at the `commit-msg` stage — after you write your commit message but before the commit is created. If the message doesn't match the format, the commit is rejected with an error message.

Source: [`jessedhillon/conventional-msg`](https://github.com/jessedhillon/conventional-msg)
